{ A central pawn break. Push to d5 and attack the pawn holding his pieces back. [%cal Yd4d5] } 19. d5 { You challenge e6 while your rooks and bishop are ready to use the open lines. [%cal Gd4d5] } (19. c5 { Also strong. You gain space and restrict the bishop instead of opening the center. } 19... Rh6 { Also playable. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Bf6h6] }) (19. Nxf7 { Also strong. You exchange the central knight rather than break with the pawn. } 19... Rxf7 { His rook takes, keeping queen and king in place. This trade is also playable. [%cal Bf6f7] }) (19. Rd2 { Also sound. Your rook chooses a different task. } 19... Rd8 { Also playable. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Be8d8] }) (19. Qe3 { Also sound. Your queen chooses a different task. } 19... Rd8 { Also playable. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Be8d8] }) (19. Qg3 { Also sound. Your queen chooses a different task. } 19... Bc8 { Also playable. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Bd7c8] }) (19. Nxd7 { You remove the bishop, but your own knight leaves its powerful post. } 19... Qxd7 20. Qe3 20... f4 { This leaves you worse off. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Rf5f4] }) 19... Nxe5 { Taking d5 invites your bishop to c4. He trades knights. Use your rook on e5. [%cal Ye2e5] } 20. Rxe5 { Your rook stays active on e5. His e6 pawn still ties down his pieces. [%cal Ge2e5] } (20. d6 { This gives too much away. Your pawn chooses a different task. } 20... Qxd6 21. Rxe5 21... c5 { This leaves you worse off. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Rc6c5] }) (20. dxe6 { This gives too much away. Your pawn chooses a different task. } 20... Rxe6 21. Bxf5 21... Rh6 { This leaves you worse off. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Re6h6] }) (20. Bc2 { This gives too much away. Your bishop chooses a different task. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { This leaves you worse off. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Rg6f4] }) (20. Bb1 { This gives too much away. Your bishop chooses a different task. } 20... Ng6 21. dxe6 21... Nf4 { This leaves you worse off. Our d pawn attacks the fixed pawn on e6. [%cal Rg6f4] }) 1-0


